\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1~\cite{deepseekr1} and QwQ~\cite{qwq} scale inference-time computation through extended chains of thought (CoT). Recent work documents that these models can ``self-jailbreak'': without adversarial input modification, the CoT transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation~\cite{harmthoughts,selfjailbreak}. We define the first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases as the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP). Existing hidden-state (HS) safety detectors~\cite{trajguard,safeswitch} classify adversarial inputs at the token level, while text monitors~\cite{saferemind} scan for explicit violations; neither tracks the gradual, multi-step commitment process. Can current probing methods reliably distinguish pre-CP from post-CP reasoning steps, and under what conditions?\footnote{This paper provides \emph{retrospective} classification, not online detection; false positive rates remain 11--50\%, so deployment is future work.}\looseness=-1

HS probes require choosing which transformer layer to monitor, a researcher degree of freedom typically treated as a minor implementation detail. We show this choice alone can reverse a study's central conclusion (\cref{fig:concept_overview}): a crossing-rate composite metric selects shallow layers (L0--L10) while threshold-based metrics (precision, BA) select deep layers (L14--L63), producing a 14-layer to 61-layer divergence; at threshold-based layers the HS advantage is +19pp ($p{<}0.005$), while at crossing-rate layers it vanishes ($p{=}0.83$). The mechanism is cosine compression: shallow representations compress directional information, inflating threshold crossings near the noise floor. At correctly selected deep layers, the HS advantage is graded: cross-validated at 7--8B (+20--22pp), significant at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15pp, below minimum detectable effect, MDE). We study four models across two architectures and two scales (\cref{tab:models}).\looseness=-1

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/fig_concept_overview.pdf}
\caption{Metric-induced layer selection reversal. The same hidden states, evaluated with different layer selection metrics, yield opposite conclusions about the HS advantage. Crossing-rate composite selects shallow layers (L0--L10), where HS and text probes perform equivalently ($p{=}0.83$); threshold-based metrics select deep layers (L14--L63), where HS probes significantly outperform text (+20pp, $p{<}0.005$). The 14--61 layer gap arises from the metric alone.}
\label{fig:concept_overview}
\end{figure*}

Our contributions are threefold. \textbf{(1)~Metric-induced layer selection bias}: CR-composite metrics systematically select shallow layers across all four models and both datasets (HarmThoughts, AdvBench), producing a qualitative reversal ($p{=}0.83$ vs.\ $p{<}0.005$) whose severity (14--61 layer divergence) has not been documented in the probing literature. \textbf{(2)~Boundary conditions}: the HS advantage is encoder-conditional (4/4 to 1/4 significant with stronger encoders) and partially overlaps with temporal position. \textbf{(3)~Negative results}: seven alternative methods all fail; control task selectivity (11--17pp above chance) confirms genuine safety structure.\looseness=-1

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought transitions from safe deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}. Self-jailbreak has been documented with step-level annotations~\cite{harmthoughts,selfjailbreak}; unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful,turpin2023language} motivates hidden-state monitoring. Safety-aligned models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience sharp drops at output generation~\cite{yin2025refusal_cliff,hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak,hu2026refusal_dynamics,ahrend2026safer_traces}. Mitigation strategies include reasoning steering~\cite{safethink} and guardrail insertion~\cite{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning}; none conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Hidden-State Probing.}
Internal activations encode safety-relevant information including alignment signals~\cite{zhou2024alignment}, refusal directions~\cite{arditi2024refusal}, and separate harmfulness/refusal encodings~\cite{zhao2025harmfulness,wollschlager2025geometry}. Monitoring systems (TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard}, SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch}, and others~\cite{siren,safedream2026,jiang2025hiddendetect,safeint2025,rcs2026,zhou2025ghost}) assume external input manipulation at the token level; the self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. Concurrently, \citet{chan2025probing} show final-layer linear probes predict alignment outcomes with strong F1, but their single-layer, single-metric evaluation does not examine whether different layers yield different conclusions. Multi-layer approaches complement our work: CLAP~\citep{clap2025} uses cross-layer attention bypassing single-layer selection, and \citet{meyoyan2026bertology} find safety features distributed across layers, echoing our observation that different metrics select different layers. We identify the mechanism (cosine compression) that makes single-layer readout unreliable; they propose architectural solutions.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Linear probing spans layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding}, sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing}, and surveys~\cite{belinkov2022probing,rogers2020primer}; control tasks~\cite{hewitt2019control} distinguish structure from memorization (adopted in \cref{app:control}). Non-uniform layer-wise information distribution~\cite{tenney2019bert,marks2024geometry} and cross-layer disagreement~\cite{kaya2019shallow,zhou2020bert} are well established; our contribution is that the known layer-wise gradient interacts with metric choice to produce systematic selection bias whose severity (14--61 layer divergence, sign reversal) exceeds what prior work documents. \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show probes can rely on textual evidence; our analysis provides a complementary geometric mechanism. We also evaluate BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian}, CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering}, and RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} as alternative temporal detectors (\cref{sec:negative}); all fail to match supervised probing.\looseness=-1

